 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of lifting steps $M$ and the spatial decomposition levels [S1, S2]. The lifting scheme is efficient, requiring $O(N)$ operations for $N$ samples per step [G].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The lifting scheme allows in-place computation or minimum memory requirement as it processes two lines at a time [S2].
-*   **Performance:** In the Leo sequence experiment (128x80x86 resolution), the depth-plane reduction approach reached a best achievable quality of 33.08 dB at 0.63 bpv, compared to 1.25 bpv for the traditional approach [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Exploits inter-component correlations effectively [S2]. Compatible with JPEG2000 Part-1 (EBCOT) for spatial coding [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** The axial DWT does not maximize coding gain compared to KLT in some volumetric contexts, but avoids KLT's drawbacks [S1]. JPEG2000 JP3D (Part-10) might offer better performance but was in Working Draft stage at the time of the sources [S2].
+*   **Compression Performance:** The method achieves significant bitrate reduction compared to traditional approaches. In the Leo sequence experiment (128x80x86 resolution), the depth-plane reduction approach reached 33.08 dB PSNR at 0.63 bpv, compared to 1.25 bpv for the traditional approach [S2].
+*   **Efficiency:** For dense data, wavelet methods (especially JPEG2000) are more effective than dictionary-based methods and RLE [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Speed:** Decompression speed for wavelet methods is generally slower than BZIP2 or RLE, but BZIP2 offers higher compression ratios for some data types [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Scalability:** Supports progressive transmission and multi-resolution representation, allowing lossy-to-lossless scalability [S1][S4, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** The method may suffer from inability to access individual slices without decoding all slices in the volumetric image, depending on the specific implementation [S1].
 
 ## Background
-*   **Origin:** The method is based on the lifting scheme formulation of the DWT as applied in JPEG2000 [S1]. It extends this to volumetric data by treating the volume as a multi-component signal, leveraging JPEG2000 Part-2 (multi-component transformation) and Part-1 (EBCOT) [S2].
-*   **Development:** The lifting scheme allows for perfect reversibility and integer-to-integer transforms, which is crucial for lossless compression of medical and volumetric data [S1]. The specific application to 3D volumes with multi-component transformation was proposed to improve compression efficiency by removing correlations among consecutive components (e.g., Y, U, V) [S2].
-*   **Usage:** Used in compression of volumetric medical image data [S1] and realistic view synthesis applications [S2]. Wavelet methods are often applied to lossless compression of medical data, treating 3D medical data (CT, MRI) as a collection of 2D grayscale images [S3, unofficial]. Implemented multi-resolution compression and progressive transmission for medical data using JPEG2000 and JPIP [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Origin:** The method is based on the lifting scheme for DWT, originally developed for JPEG2000 [S1]. The specific application to 3D volumes with multi-component transformation leverages JPEG2000 Part-2 (multi-component) and Part-1 (EBCOT) standards [S2].
+*   **History:** Developed in the context of compressing hyper-spectral and volumetric medical image data (CT, MRI) to provide reversible compression and reduce computational complexity compared to KLT [S1].
+*   **Usage:** Implemented for multi-resolution compression and progressive transmission of medical data using JPEG2000 and JPIP [S4, unofficial].
 
